<blockquote>
<p>The original intent for the story was that I was going to leave it open-ended; the beginning essentially takes real-world recent history and views it through a fantastic lens. The idea was that the stalemate is still ongoing but appears to be losing effectiveness as time progresses (utilizing events such as Japan's birthrate reaching its lowest recorded number last year and America's marriage rates reaching a record low the year before as evidence).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My concern with this is that the open-ended nature here feels unsatisfying: the truce slowly falling apart feels&#8230;like a fine progression but I think it'd feel like it was missing closure if we didn't see how it all ended?</p>
<p>I also have some concerns about the progression through this: it feels&#8230;perhaps too loose? Like, in the sense that, I don't really have a clear idea of what the in article discussion is as the effectiveness is lost or like, what stance the various members of the stalemate are taking or anything.</p>
<p>Having tales building off this isn't a terrible idea but it feels&#8230;perhaps like they might feel stronger if included in the SCP?</p>

